1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,150
Imagine a flying machine that looks
almost exactly like a helicopter.

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:06,990
It has a big rotor on top.

3
00:00:07,773 --> 00:00:11,103
It can take off and land in
a surprisingly small space.

4
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:15,463
And if its engine stops in
mid-air, it can still land safely.

5
00:00:16,361 --> 00:00:19,571
Sounds like a helicopter,
right? Well… not quite.

6
00:00:20,301 --> 00:00:22,691
This is a gyroplane.
Also called an autogyro.

7
00:00:24,105 --> 00:00:26,055
And the weirdest thing about it is this.

8
00:00:26,605 --> 00:00:28,775
The engine doesn't power the rotor.

9
00:00:31,555 --> 00:00:34,525
In a helicopter, the engine
spins the main rotor.

10
00:00:35,675 --> 00:00:38,955
The rotor produces lift,
and by tilting the rotor,

11
00:00:39,035 --> 00:00:41,645
the helicopter can also move
in different directions.

12
00:00:42,619 --> 00:00:47,039
But there is a problem. If you spin
a rotor one way, physics wants to

13
00:00:47,119 --> 00:00:49,449
spin the rest of the
helicopter the other way.

14
00:00:50,205 --> 00:00:54,605
That's why conventional helicopters
need a way to counteract that torque.

15
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:56,435
Usually a tail rotor.

16
00:00:57,364 --> 00:00:59,594
A gyroplane doesn't have this problem.

17
00:01:00,404 --> 00:01:03,254
Its engine powers a
completely separate propeller.

18
00:01:03,969 --> 00:01:06,219
The propeller pushes the aircraft forward.

19
00:01:06,569 --> 00:01:09,559
And the airflow through the
big rotor on top makes it spin.

20
00:01:10,169 --> 00:01:11,479
This is called autorotation.

21
00:01:13,283 --> 00:01:14,603
So the basic idea is this.

22
00:01:14,683 --> 00:01:19,613
Engine, propeller, forward motion,
airflow, spinning rotor, lift.

23
00:01:20,442 --> 00:01:21,642
But hold on.

24
00:01:22,042 --> 00:01:25,492
Why should air flowing through a
rotor make that rotor spin at all?

25
00:01:27,782 --> 00:01:29,232
The answer is the angle.

26
00:01:30,002 --> 00:01:32,872
The rotor disc isn't
flat. It's tilted back.

27
00:01:33,642 --> 00:01:37,012
So the air isn't pushed down through
it, the way it is under a helicopter.

28
00:01:37,882 --> 00:01:39,432
It comes up through it instead.

29
00:01:40,385 --> 00:01:41,875
Now think about one blade.

30
00:01:42,805 --> 00:01:44,745
It's slicing forward
through the air, and at

31
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:47,335
the same time, air is arriving from below.

32
00:01:48,185 --> 00:01:51,795
The flow it meets is the sum of
both, and it comes from underneath.

33
00:01:52,645 --> 00:01:55,175
That tips the lift of the
blade slightly forward.

34
00:01:55,645 --> 00:01:58,655
Forward, in the direction
the blade is already moving.

35
00:01:59,421 --> 00:02:01,991
So the air isn't only
holding the rotor up.

36
00:02:02,401 --> 00:02:03,891
It's driving it around.

37
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:08,381
Though not the whole blade does that.

38
00:02:08,461 --> 00:02:11,591
Where the flow arrives
steeply, the blade is driven.

39
00:02:12,121 --> 00:02:14,431
Where it arrives shallowly, it drags.

40
00:02:15,214 --> 00:02:18,224
Nobody is holding the rotor
at any particular speed.

41
00:02:19,114 --> 00:02:20,854
It settles where those two balance, and

42
00:02:20,934 --> 00:02:23,324
holds there by itself
for the whole flight.

43
00:02:26,284 --> 00:02:28,274
The engine doesn't have to spin the rotor.

44
00:02:29,084 --> 00:02:30,474
The air does it for free.

45
00:02:31,404 --> 00:02:33,354
Well… not completely for free.

46
00:02:34,115 --> 00:02:36,195
You are exchanging some forward speed for

47
00:02:36,275 --> 00:02:38,645
the energy needed to
keep the rotor spinning.

48
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:41,445
But the important point is this.

49
00:02:42,155 --> 00:02:44,385
There is no engine-driven rotor shaft.

50
00:02:45,245 --> 00:02:47,415
So why doesn't the whole aircraft spin?

51
00:02:48,185 --> 00:02:50,615
This is probably the
first thing you would ask.

52
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:55,710
If the rotor is spinning, shouldn't the
aircraft spin in the opposite direction?

53
00:02:56,660 --> 00:02:58,650
And yes, there can be aerodynamic torques.

54
00:03:00,033 --> 00:03:02,533
But there is a huge difference
between a rotor being driven

55
00:03:02,613 --> 00:03:06,223
by an engine, and a rotor
being driven by the airflow.

56
00:03:07,169 --> 00:03:10,009
In a helicopter, the engine
continuously supplies

57
00:03:10,089 --> 00:03:13,639
torque to overcome the
aerodynamic drag of the rotor.

58
00:03:14,539 --> 00:03:16,529
The rotor pushes against the air.

59
00:03:16,779 --> 00:03:18,529
The air pushes back.

60
00:03:18,819 --> 00:03:21,509
And that reaction torque
has to be counteracted.

61
00:03:22,405 --> 00:03:25,315
In a gyroplane, the
rotor is free-spinning.

62
00:03:25,825 --> 00:03:28,255
It isn't being forced
to rotate by the engine.

63
00:03:29,065 --> 00:03:31,215
The airflow is what keeps it rotating.

64
00:03:32,169 --> 00:03:34,829
So there is no large engine
torque being transmitted

65
00:03:34,909 --> 00:03:37,059
through the rotor shaft into the aircraft.

66
00:03:38,046 --> 00:03:42,076
And that's why you don't need a tail rotor
just to keep the fuselage from spinning.

67
00:03:45,006 --> 00:03:46,206
But there's a catch.

68
00:03:46,846 --> 00:03:50,436
If you're thinking, "so this is
just a cheap helicopter"… not quite.

69
00:03:51,333 --> 00:03:55,303
The gyroplane has one enormous
limitation. It can't hover.

70
00:03:58,163 --> 00:04:01,633
A helicopter can generate lift
while sitting almost stationary.

71
00:04:02,723 --> 00:04:05,193
A gyroplane needs airflow
through its rotor.

72
00:04:06,091 --> 00:04:08,501
That means it normally
has to move forward.

73
00:04:09,351 --> 00:04:13,581
So unlike a helicopter, you can't simply
point it upward and take off vertically.

74
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:17,910
You accelerate along the ground,
the rotor spins faster and

75
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:21,500
faster, and eventually there
is enough lift to take off.

76
00:04:22,263 --> 00:04:24,783
Some gyroplanes can
pre-spin their rotors before

77
00:04:24,863 --> 00:04:28,213
takeoff, allowing a
very short takeoff run.

78
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:31,120
And that spinning up can be taken further.

79
00:04:32,030 --> 00:04:36,220
Far enough that a few gyroplanes can
leave the ground without any run at all.

80
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:38,370
It's called a jump takeoff.

81
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:43,130
The rotor is spun up on the ground
past its normal flying speed,

82
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:46,910
with the blades set almost flat,
so they make hardly any lift.

83
00:04:47,686 --> 00:04:50,656
Then the pilot changes the
blade angle all at once.

84
00:04:51,546 --> 00:04:54,966
The stored spin turns into lift,
and the machine hops off the ground.

85
00:04:55,046 --> 00:04:58,036
Cierva's machines could
clear about six metres.

86
00:04:59,562 --> 00:05:02,812
But it's one jump, out of
energy the rotor already had.

87
00:05:03,602 --> 00:05:07,652
The moment it's up, it still has to
fly forward to keep that rotor turning.

88
00:05:08,402 --> 00:05:09,972
It doesn't hover at the top.

89
00:05:10,734 --> 00:05:12,304
But it's still not a helicopter.

90
00:05:12,954 --> 00:05:17,204
It's much closer to a strange hybrid
between an aeroplane and a helicopter.

91
00:05:20,174 --> 00:05:22,024
What happens when the engine stops?

92
00:05:22,734 --> 00:05:25,324
Here's where the gyroplane
gets really interesting.

93
00:05:26,354 --> 00:05:29,564
Imagine you're flying along…
and the engine suddenly dies.

94
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:32,800
In an aeroplane, you now have a glider.

95
00:05:33,690 --> 00:05:36,210
In a helicopter, the
pilot enters autorotation

96
00:05:36,290 --> 00:05:39,280
and uses the airflow to
keep the rotor spinning.

97
00:05:40,127 --> 00:05:43,117
And in a gyroplane? You're
already in autorotation.

98
00:05:46,462 --> 00:05:49,512
The rotor is normally powered
by the airflow anyway.

99
00:05:50,302 --> 00:05:54,052
So the engine can stop turning the
propeller… but the rotor keeps spinning.

100
00:05:54,859 --> 00:05:58,449
The pilot can control the descent
and make an emergency landing.

101
00:05:59,319 --> 00:06:01,089
Of course, this isn't magic.

102
00:06:01,954 --> 00:06:06,944
You still need altitude, speed, a suitable
landing area and a competent pilot.

103
00:06:07,895 --> 00:06:09,675
And it isn't crash-proof.

104
00:06:10,195 --> 00:06:13,125
The rotor turns because the
air is pushing up through it.

105
00:06:13,755 --> 00:06:15,505
Unload it, and it slows down.

106
00:06:16,230 --> 00:06:18,750
Older designs carried the
propeller high above the

107
00:06:18,830 --> 00:06:22,020
centre of gravity, and
could pitch down into it.

108
00:06:22,610 --> 00:06:25,310
Modern machines are built against it, and

109
00:06:25,390 --> 00:06:27,740
pilots are taught never
to unload the rotor.

110
00:06:28,659 --> 00:06:31,219
But an engine failure doesn't
automatically mean that

111
00:06:31,299 --> 00:06:34,409
the aircraft has lost its
ability to generate lift.

112
00:06:37,239 --> 00:06:39,189
And this thing is surprisingly old.

113
00:06:39,899 --> 00:06:42,579
You might think gyroplanes are
some modern ultralight invention.

114
00:06:42,659 --> 00:06:44,609
They're not.

115
00:06:45,422 --> 00:06:48,162
The first successful
practical gyroplane was

116
00:06:48,242 --> 00:06:51,012
developed by Spanish
engineer Juan de la Cierva.

117
00:06:52,110 --> 00:06:56,253
His C four made its first successful
flight in nineteen twenty-three.

118
00:06:56,510 --> 00:06:58,500
That's more than a century ago.

119
00:06:59,269 --> 00:07:02,649
And Cierva's work on rotor
systems also contributed ideas

120
00:07:02,729 --> 00:07:06,579
that later became important
in helicopter development.

121
00:07:07,493 --> 00:07:12,303
So the gyroplane wasn't invented as a
cheap alternative to modern helicopters.

122
00:07:13,307 --> 00:07:14,887
It was actually one of the stepping stones

123
00:07:14,967 --> 00:07:18,057
toward rotary-wing flight
as we know it today.

124
00:07:21,047 --> 00:07:23,257
And this thing is surprisingly affordable.

125
00:07:24,347 --> 00:07:27,027
A modern two-seat gyroplane
can cost only a fraction

126
00:07:27,107 --> 00:07:29,737
of the price of a
comparable small helicopter.

127
00:07:30,681 --> 00:07:34,431
Depending on the model, a gyroplane
can be available for roughly twenty

128
00:07:34,511 --> 00:07:38,691
to forty per cent of the purchase
price of a comparable helicopter.

129
00:07:39,673 --> 00:07:41,463
And the difference doesn't end there.

130
00:07:42,353 --> 00:07:45,733
A small gyroplane might burn
roughly ten to twenty litres of fuel

131
00:07:45,813 --> 00:07:49,903
per hour, depending on the engine,
the aircraft and the conditions.

132
00:07:51,013 --> 00:07:55,173
At a cruising speed around one hundred
and forty kilometres per hour, that

133
00:07:55,253 --> 00:07:58,323
can work out to roughly seven to
fourteen litres per hundred kilometres.

134
00:08:00,027 --> 00:08:04,247
That's not exactly economical
transport… but for a machine with a

135
00:08:04,327 --> 00:08:08,237
rotor on top that flies through
the sky, it's pretty remarkable.

136
00:08:09,155 --> 00:08:12,005
So if you simply want to
fly, and don't need to hover…

137
00:08:12,515 --> 00:08:15,365
a gyroplane starts looking
surprisingly attractive.

138
00:08:18,125 --> 00:08:20,115
So why doesn't everyone fly one?

139
00:08:20,785 --> 00:08:22,595
And this is the real question.

140
00:08:23,345 --> 00:08:25,575
The gyroplane is relatively simple.

141
00:08:26,353 --> 00:08:28,363
It doesn't need a powered main rotor.

142
00:08:29,253 --> 00:08:32,143
It can survive an engine
failure remarkably well.

143
00:08:32,673 --> 00:08:34,943
It can fly at respectable speeds.

144
00:08:35,904 --> 00:08:40,134
And compared with a helicopter, it can
be much cheaper to buy and to operate.

145
00:08:41,099 --> 00:08:44,749
But you give up the helicopter's
most useful trick. Hovering.

146
00:08:47,459 --> 00:08:51,779
You need forward speed. You need
space for takeoff and landing.

147
00:08:51,859 --> 00:08:55,369
And you can't simply land
vertically in your back garden.

148
00:08:56,113 --> 00:09:00,043
So the gyroplane isn't a cheap
helicopter. It's something different.

149
00:09:00,573 --> 00:09:04,603
It's an aircraft that takes one of the
most useful ideas from the helicopter.

150
00:09:05,413 --> 00:09:06,613
The rotor.

151
00:09:06,963 --> 00:09:10,213
And combines it with one of
the simplest ideas in aviation.

152
00:09:11,403 --> 00:09:13,573
A propeller pushing the aircraft forward.

153
00:09:14,379 --> 00:09:18,869
And somehow, this strange combination has
been flying for more than a hundred years.
